{"data": [{"author_id": "3591710966", "created_at": "2026-10-29T04:05:21.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2115656208900034951"], "entities": {"hashtags": [{"end": 19, "start": 2, "tag": "HighlyCitedPaper"}, {"end": 148, "start": 131, "tag": "SodiumIonBattery"}, {"end": 176, "start": 149, "tag": "AcceleratedRateCalorimetry"}, {"end": 191, "start": 177, "tag": "SafetyTesting"}, {"end": 208, "start": 192, "tag": "CommercialCells"}, {"end": 223, "start": 210, "tag": "mdpienergies"}, {"end": 235, "start": 224, "tag": "openaccess"}], "urls": [{"description": "Herein, we investigate the performance and safety of four of the early-stage, commercial Na-ion batteries available in 2024, representing the most popular cathode types across research and commercialization: polyanion (Na-VPF), layered metal oxide (Na-NMF), and a Prussian blue analog (Na-tmCN). The cells deliver a wide range of energy density with Na-tmCN delivering the least (23 Wh/kg) and Na-NMF delivering the most (127 Wh/kg). The Na-VPF cell was in between (47 Wg/kg). Capacity retention under specified cycling conditions and with periodic 0 V excursions was the most robust for the Na-tmCN cells in both cases. Accelerating rate calorimetry (ARC) and nail penetration testing finds that Na-NMF cells do undergo thermal runaway in response to abuse, while the Na-VPF and Na-tmCN exhibit only low self-heating rates (<1 °C/min). During these safety tests, all cells exhibited off-gassing, so we conducted in-line FTIR equipped with a heated gas cell to detect CO, CO2, CH4, toxic acid gases (", "display_url": "brnw.ch/21x6KcO", "end": 128, "expanded_url": "https://brnw.ch/21x6KcO", "images": [{"height": 630, "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/news_img/2115656220816076800/r2bbdfTu?format=jpg&name=orig", "width": 1200}, {"height": 150, "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/news_img/2115656220816076800/r2bbdfTu?format=jpg&name=150x150", "width": 150}], "start": 105, "status": 200, "title": "First Look at Safety and Performance Evaluation of Commercial Sodium-Ion Batteries", "unwound_url": "https://www.mdpi.com/1996-1073/18/3/661?utm_source=twitter&utm_medium=social_journ&utm_campaign=energies", "url": "https://t.co/A2kkaC66IC"}, {"display_url": "pic.x.com/EFLTLhacq1", "end": 259, "expanded_url": "https://x.com/energies_mdpi/status/2115656208900034951/photo/1", "media_key": "3_2115656205691502592", "start": 236, "url": "https://t.co/EFLTLhacq1"}]}, "id": "2115656208900034951", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 74, "like_count": 1, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 1}, "text": "✨ #HighlyCitedPaper First Look at Safety and Performance Evaluation of Commercial Sodium-Ion Batteries\n👉 https://t.co/A2kkaC66IC \n\n#SodiumIonBattery #AcceleratedRateCalorimetry #SafetyTesting #CommercialCells\n\n#mdpienergies #openaccess https://t.co/EFLTLhacq1", "url": "https://x.com/energies_mdpi/status/2115656208900034951"}, {"author_id": "783719151684685824", "created_at": "2026-10-28T21:04:45.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2115550359271702804"], "entities": {"urls": [{"description": "Chinese battery giant Contemporary Amperex Technology Co. Ltd. is working to turn growing Australian interest in sodium-ion batteries into project investments as demand for energy-storage systems grows.", "display_url": "bloomberg.com/news/articles/…", "end": 189, "expanded_url": "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-28/catl-sees-australian-interest-in-nascent-sodium-ion-batteries?taid=6ae2636d25edfb00017cf132&utm_campaign=trueanthem&utm_content=climate&utm_medium=social&utm_source=twitter", "images": [{"height": 675, "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/news_img/2115550363407269888/eDYmnrI6?format=png&name=orig", "width": 1200}, {"height": 150, "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/news_img/2115550363407269888/eDYmnrI6?format=png&name=150x150", "width": 150}], "start": 166, "status": 200, "title": "CATL Sees Australian Interest in Nascent Sodium-Ion Batteries", "unwound_url": "https://www.bloom